See LexicalBinding for context.

You can use the shell script embedded below (n.b. tested with GNU utils only) to add lexical-binding cookies to *.el files which do not already have one on their first line.  This page assumes that you have saved it as 'lexel' to a directory in your PATH, and have made it executable.

WARNING: The default behaviour of this script is to run a recursive search for lisp files which lack the cookie, and modify those files.  You should use the --dry-run option until you are sure that the files it will modify are (only) the files that you want it to modify, and you should test it against a copy of the files first.

For help, run:

 lexel --help

It will apply recursively from the current directory if no arguments are given, or recursively over the specified files and directories.  E.g.:

 lexel ~/.emacs.d/elpa ~/.emacs.d/user-lisp

Do not do this directly on ~/.emacs.d itself.  That directory should not be in your load-path and so, other than the init file(s) recognised by Emacs, you should not have any lisp files directly in that directory (as Emacs may write *.el files there which are data rather than lisp code, which we will not want to touch here).  You should instead run the script against the specific (sub-)directories where any *.el files should all be lisp code.

(If you do have other lisp files directly in ~/.emacs.d then you can target them explicitly, but you should move them elsewhere -- possibly to the new (info "(emacs)User Lisp Directory") introduced in Emacs 31.)

Take a backup first, of course, and I recommend testing against a copy so you can inspect the results and compare with your original config, before running it against the real directories.

The script outputs the list of the files that it has modified (or at least attempted to modify -- it may not have permission to do so), so that you can easily check those results.

==Shell script==

 #!/bin/sh
 # Lexel
 # Version 1.0
 # Author: Phil S.
 # For usage information, run: lexel --help
 lex=nil
 force=nil
 help=
 dry_run=
 
 opts=$(getopt -o hf -l nil,t,help,force,dry-run -a -n "$(basename "$0")" -- "$@")
 error=$?
 
 if [ "${error}" != "0" ]; then
     printf "\n"
     short_help=1
 else
     eval set -- ${opts}
     for param; do
         case $param in
             --t ) lex=t; shift;;
             --nil ) lex=nil; shift;;
             --force | -f ) force=t; shift;;
             --help | -h ) help=1; shift;;
             --dry-run ) dry_run=1; shift;;
             -- ) shift; break;;
         esac
     done
 fi
 
 if [ -n "${short_help}" ] || [ -n "${help}" ]; then
     cat <<EOF
 Usage:
 $(basename "$0") [-nil|-t] [-f|--force] [--dry-run] [-h|--help] [--] [PATHS...]
 
 EOF
 
     if [ -n "${help}" ]; then
         cat <<EOF
 Adds a -*- lexical-binding -*- cookie to every *.el file within PATHS which
 does not have one already.  If PATHS is omitted then the current directory
 is used.  Directories are processed recursively to find *.el files.
 
 The cookie is added as -*- lexical-binding: nil; -*- by default, so that the
 behaviour of the lisp code is not changed.
 
 OPTIONS
 
   -nil
         Set -*- lexical-binding: nil; -*-.  This is the default, so this
         argument is optional.
 
   -t
         Set -*- lexical-binding: t; -*-.  This might necessitate additional
         changes to the files to properly convert them to lexical-binding.
 
   -f, --force
         Also process files with an existing cookie (as well as those without),
         replacing the old lexical-binding values with the new value.
 
   --dry-run
         Show the files which would be modifed without making any changes.
 
   -h, --help
         Display this help.
 
   --
         Marks the end of the options, in case any PATHS begin with a hypen.
 
   PATHS...
         The files and directories to process.  Files matching ".dir-locals*"
         or "*-pkg.el" are always skipped (even if --force is used).
 EOF
     fi
     exit ${error-0}
 fi
 
 if [ "${force}" = "t" ]; then
     # Retain all found files.
     filter() {
         cat
     }
 else
     # Filter out files which already have a cookie.
     filter() {
         xargs -0r -L1 sed -n -e '1{/\blexical-binding\b/!F}' \
             | tr '\n' '\0'
     }
 fi
 
 if [ -n "${dry_run}" ]; then
     process() {
         xargs -0r printf "%s\n"
     }
 else
     process() {
         xargs -0r emacs -Q --batch --eval '(add-hook (quote find-file-hook)
           (lambda ()
             (when (eq major-mode (quote emacs-lisp-mode))
               (let* ((prop-vars (hack-local-variables-prop-line))
                      (cookie (assq (quote lexical-binding) prop-vars)))
                 (when (or '"${force}"' (not cookie))
                   (princ (format "%s\n" buffer-file-name))
                   (add-file-local-variable-prop-line (quote lexical-binding)
                                                      '"${lex}"')
                   (save-buffer))))))' --
     }
 fi
 
 #set -x
 find "${@:-.}" -type f \( \
      \( \( -name ".dir-locals*" -o -name "*-pkg.el" \) -prune \) \
      -o \( -name "*.el" -print0 \) \) \
     | filter \
     | process
